```python
@app.post("/charge")
def charge_card(request):
    amount = request.json["amount"]
    result = payment_gateway.charge(request.user.card, amount)
    return {"status": "ok", "charge_id": result.id}
```
If the client times out waiting for a response and retries, this endpoint has no way to know it's seeing the same logical request twice — it just charges the card again. The client did the reasonable thing (retry on timeout); the endpoint is what's unsafe.

```python
@app.post("/charge")
def charge_card(request):
    key = request.headers["Idempotency-Key"]
    if idempotency_store.has(key):
        return idempotency_store.get(key)          # short-circuit the retry
    amount = request.json["amount"]
    result = payment_gateway.charge(request.user.card, amount)
    response = {"status": "ok", "charge_id": result.id}
    idempotency_store.save(key, response)
    return response
```
The client generates `Idempotency-Key` once and reuses it on every retry of the *same* logical charge. Now a retried request returns the original result instead of charging the card a second time.
